  "text": "Sure! Let's break down the task:\n\nWe need to write a Python function called `word_histogram` that:\n\n1. Takes a string as input.\n2. Returns a dictionary where each key is a **word** (case-insensitive) and the value is the **frequency** of that word.\n3. **Ignore punctuation** and **case** in the input string.\n\n### Steps to solve:\n- Normalize the input: convert to lowercase.\n- Remove punctuation.\n- Split the string into words.\n- Count the frequency of each word using a dictionary or `collections.Counter`.\n\nHere's the implementation:\n\n```python\nimport string\n\ndef word_histogram(text):\n    # Convert text to lowercase\n    text = text.lower()\n    \n    # Remove punctuation\n    text = text.translate(str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation))\n    \n    # Split the text into words\n    words = text.split()\n    \n    # Count frequency of each word\n    histogram = {}\n    for word in words:\n        histogram[word] = histogram.get(word, 0) + 1\n    \n    return histogram\n```\n\n### How it works:\n- `text.lower()` makes all letters lowercase.\n- `str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation)` creates a translation table to remove punctuation.\n- `text.translate(...)` strips punctuation from the string.\n- `split()` splits the text into words based on whitespace.\n- We use a dictionary to count the frequency of each word.\n\n### Unit Tests:\n```python\nassert word_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\") == {\"hello\": 2, \"world\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\") == {\"python\": 2, \"is\": 2, \"great\": 1, \"fun\": 1}\nassert word_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\") == {\"test\": 3}\n```\n\nLet me know if you want a version using `collections.Counter` or any other language like JavaScript!",
